    {
      "user_claim_id": "c2",
      "disposition": "supports",
      "observation_ids": ["img-k7-o1", "img-v9-o0", "img-q2-o1", "img-v9-o1"],
      "discriminative_explanation": "Multiple observations explicitly link the appearance of folds to the material's properties rather than external manipulation. Observation img-k7-o1 states the material is 'carved... to drape' and 'shaped with extreme subtlety to suggest a flexible textile.' Observation img-v9-o0 notes that the matte, diffuse light interaction 'implies rigidity within the folds rather than the fluid drape of fabric,' suggesting the folds are an intrinsic property of the solid material. Observation img-q2-o1 describes the texture as 'soft and pliable, suggesting a fabric or cloth that has been carefully molded.' These observations favor the stance that the folding is a result of how the material is carved or molded ('one thing the material does') over the stance that it is an external addition like real cloth.",
      "unresolved_alternatives": "The observations confirm the *appearance* is that of folding caused by the material's form, but they cannot rule out that a separate layer of actual fabric was attached to the bust and then photographed, as the 'apparent material effect' is always labeled as 'interpretive' and some observations note the lack of visible seams or confirmation of a single piece."
