    {
      "user_claim_id": "c3",
      "disposition": "supports",
      "observation_ids": ["img-k7-o0", "img-k7-o1", "img-v9-o0", "img-q2-o0", "img-q2-o1", "img-v9-o2"],
      "discriminative_explanation": "The observations consistently attribute the visual effects of the folds to lighting and surface interaction rather than differences in carving technique. For instance, img-k7-o1 explains the 'play of bright highlights and dark valleys' as light catching convex curves through folds. Img-v9-o0 explicitly states that the way light interacts (matte, diffuse scattering) creates the definition of the folds without harsh reflections. Img-q2-o1 notes that light behaves as if striking a 'matte surface with low reflectivity,' creating high contrast. These descriptions favor the stance that the *differences* in how the folds look (shadows, highlights, depth) are caused by lighting conditions and the material's reflectivity, rather than the carving itself being different.",
      "unresolved_alternatives": "While lighting explains the *visual rendering* of the folds, it does not explain the *existence* of the folds themselves. The stance that differences are caused *only* by lighting would imply the folds are identical in every photo, which contradicts claim c1. However, regarding the *description* of the folds' appearance, the observations strongly support that lighting is the primary driver of the visual variance."
